    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, '101: A patient presents with a history of recurrent epistaxis (nosebleeds). The examiner inspects the nasal septum and notes a prominent vascular plexus on the anterior septum. This area is known as:', '["Kiesselbach''s plexus", "Woodruff''s plexus", "Waldeyer''s ring", "Stensen''s duct"]', 0, 'Kiesselbach''s plexus is located on the anterior nasal septum and is the most common site for epistaxis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '102: When examining the mouth of a patient with suspected mumps, the examiner should look for swelling and erythema at the opening of the parotid duct. Where is this located?', '["Underneath the tongue on either side of the frenulum", "On the buccal mucosa opposite the second upper molar", "On the hard palate", "On the posterior pharyngeal wall"]', 1, 'The parotid (Stensen''s) duct opens onto the buccal mucosa opposite the second maxillary molar.'),
    (v_topic_id, '103: A patient has a smooth, glossy, red tongue (glossitis) with loss of papillae. This physical finding is classically associated with which nutritional deficiency?', '["Vitamin C deficiency", "Vitamin A deficiency", "Vitamin B12 or Iron deficiency", "Zinc deficiency"]', 2, 'Glossitis and angular cheilitis are classic signs of anemia, particularly due to Vitamin B12, folate, or severe iron deficiency.'),
    (v_topic_id, '104: During palpation of the neck, a hard, fixed, non-tender nodule is felt in the thyroid gland. What is the most appropriate next step in the physical examination?', '["Auscultate for a thyroid bruit", "Transilluminate the nodule", "Test for Trousseau''s sign", "Perform the Dix-Hallpike maneuver"]', 0, 'Auscultating for a bruit can help differentiate a highly vascular lesion (like a toxic adenoma or Graves) from a solid tumor, though a hard, fixed nodule is highly suspicious for malignancy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '105: A patient complains of chronic sinusitis. To assess the frontal sinuses, the examiner should apply pressure:', '["Below the eyes, on the maxillary bones", "Above the eyes, pressing up under the bony brow", "Over the bridge of the nose", "Behind the ears over the mastoid process"]', 1, 'The frontal sinuses are palpated by pressing upward under the medial aspect of the bony eyebrows.'),
    (v_topic_id, '106: When percussing the posterior thorax, normal air-filled lung tissue should produce which sound?', '["Tympany", "Hyperresonance", "Resonance", "Dullness"]', 2, 'Resonance is the normal percussion note over healthy, air-filled lung tissue.'),
    (v_topic_id, '107: A patient with pneumonia has a consolidated right lower lobe. When the patient is asked to say "ee," the examiner hears an "ay" sound through the stethoscope over the right lower lobe. This phenomenon is called:', '["Bronchophony", "Whispered pectoriloquy", "Egophony", "Tactile fremitus"]', 2, 'Egophony (E-to-A change) occurs because consolidated lung tissue preferentially transmits higher-frequency sounds, changing the "ee" vowel sound to "ay."'),
    (v_topic_id, '108: When assessing whispered pectoriloquy, the patient is asked to whisper "99." What is the expected finding over an area of lung consolidation?', '["The sound is absent", "The sound is heard faintly and indistinctly", "The sound is heard clearly and loudly", "The sound has a musical quality"]', 2, 'Consolidation transmits sound much better than air. Therefore, whispered words, which are normally faint or absent, become loud and distinct (whispered pectoriloquy).'),
    (v_topic_id, '109: A patient presents with acute dyspnea. Tracheal deviation to the LEFT is noted on physical exam. Which of the following conditions is most likely?', '["Left-sided tension pneumothorax", "Right-sided tension pneumothorax", "Left-sided lobar pneumonia", "Bilateral asthma exacerbation"]', 1, 'A tension pneumothorax builds immense pressure in the pleural space, pushing the mediastinum and trachea away from the affected side (to the contralateral side).'),
    (v_topic_id, '110: A patient with a massive left-sided pleural effusion would have tracheal deviation toward which side?', '["To the left (ipsilateral)", "To the right (contralateral)", "The trachea will remain midline", "It depends on the patient''s position"]', 1, 'A massive pleural effusion acts as a space-occupying lesion, pushing the mediastinal structures, including the trachea, to the opposite (contralateral) side.'),
    (v_topic_id, '111: Conversely, a patient with severe left-sided lung atelectasis (collapse) would have tracheal deviation toward which side?', '["To the left (ipsilateral)", "To the right (contralateral)", "The trachea will remain midline", "Tracheal deviation does not occur with atelectasis"]', 0, 'Atelectasis involves volume loss in the lung. The negative pressure created by this volume loss pulls the mediastinal structures toward the affected (ipsilateral) side.'),
    (v_topic_id, '112: Auscultation of the lungs reveals low-pitched, continuous, snoring-like sounds that clear partially after the patient coughs. These sounds are best described as:', '["Fine crackles", "Coarse crackles", "Wheezes", "Rhonchi"]', 3, 'Rhonchi are low-pitched, continuous sounds caused by secretions in the large airways (bronchi). Because they are in the airways, they often clear or change with coughing.'),
    (v_topic_id, '113: A patient in respiratory distress demonstrates inward movement of the intercostal spaces during inspiration. This physical finding is termed:', '["Paradoxical breathing", "Retractions", "Flail chest", "Kussmaul breathing"]', 1, 'Retractions indicate greatly increased work of breathing and highly negative intrathoracic pressures, pulling the soft tissues inward during inspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, '114: A patient presents following severe chest trauma. During inspiration, a segment of the right chest wall moves inward, and during expiration, it bulges outward. This is diagnostic of:', '["Tension pneumothorax", "Cardiac tamponade", "Flail chest", "Pulmonary contusion"]', 2, 'A flail chest occurs when multiple adjacent ribs are fractured in two or more places, creating a free-floating segment that moves paradoxically with respiratory pressures.'),
    (v_topic_id, '115: When assessing a patient for a deep vein thrombosis (DVT), the examiner acutely dorsiflexes the patient''s foot, which elicits pain in the calf. This is known as a positive:', '["Homan''s sign", "Pratt''s sign", "Trendelenburg sign", "Trousseau''s sign"]', 0, 'Homan''s sign (calf pain on forced dorsiflexion) is historically associated with DVT, though it is now known to be notoriously insensitive and nonspecific.'),
    (v_topic_id, '116: The measurement of the Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) is an indirect assessment of the pressure in which cardiac chamber?', '["Left atrium", "Right atrium", "Left ventricle", "Right ventricle"]', 1, 'The internal jugular vein acts as a direct manometer communicating directly with the right atrium (central venous pressure) without intervening valves.'),
    (v_topic_id, '117: When evaluating the JVP waveform, the "a" wave corresponds to:', '["Right ventricular contraction", "Opening of the tricuspid valve", "Right atrial contraction", "Bulging of the tricuspid valve into the atrium"]', 2, 'The "a" wave represents atrial contraction (atrial systole) at the end of ventricular diastole.'),
    (v_topic_id, '118: A patient has atrial fibrillation. Which wave will be absent on their JVP waveform?', '["The ''a'' wave", "The ''c'' wave", "The ''v'' wave", "The ''y'' descent"]', 0, 'Because atrial fibrillation involves chaotic atrial quivering without organized contraction, the "a" wave (which requires coordinated atrial contraction) is absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, '119: Large, bounding "cannon a waves" in the JVP are a classic physical exam sign of:', '["Atrial fibrillation", "Third-degree (complete) heart block", "Mitral regurgitation", "Aortic stenosis"]', 1, 'Cannon a waves occur when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, which happens in complete heart block when atrial and ventricular rhythms are dissociated.'),
    (v_topic_id, '120: On palpation of the precordium, the examiner feels a fine, palpable, rushing vibration. This is called a:', '["Heave", "Thrill", "Lift", "Gallop"]', 1, 'A thrill is the palpable manifestation of a loud, harsh murmur (usually grade 4/6 or higher).'),
    (v_topic_id, '121: The Point of Maximal Impulse (PMI) is normally located at:', '["The 4th intercostal space at the left sternal border", "The 5th intercostal space in the midclavicular line", "The 2nd intercostal space at the right sternal border", "The subxiphoid area"]', 1, 'The PMI corresponds to the apex of the left ventricle and is normally found in the 5th ICS at the midclavicular line.'),
    (v_topic_id, '122: A patient has long-standing hypertension leading to severe left ventricular hypertrophy. How will the PMI change on physical exam?', '["It will be displaced to the right", "It will be absent", "It will be displaced laterally and feel sustained/enlarged", "It will move to the epigastrium"]', 2, 'LVH causes the apical impulse to become forceful, sustained, and often displaced laterally (beyond the midclavicular line) and inferiorly.'),
    (v_topic_id, '123: Which heart sound is often described as having a "Kentucky" cadence and is considered pathological in older adults?', '["S1", "S2", "S3", "S4"]', 2, 'The S3 gallop follows S2 ("lub-dup-ta" or "Ken-tuck-y") and indicates volume overload and reduced ventricular compliance, highly specific for heart failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, '124: Which heart sound is often described as having a "Tennessee" cadence and is caused by atrial contraction forcing blood into a stiff, non-compliant ventricle?', '["S1", "S2", "S3", "S4"]', 3, 'The S4 gallop precedes S1 ("ta-lub-dup" or "Ten-nes-see") and indicates a stiff ventricle (e.g., from severe LVH or acute ischemia).'),
    (v_topic_id, '125: A patient with suspected peripheral arterial disease (PAD) undergoes an Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI) measurement. An ABI of 0.6 indicates:', '["Normal blood flow", "Mild to moderate PAD", "Severe ischemia (impending tissue loss)", "Incompressible, calcified vessels"]', 1, 'An ABI between 0.41 and 0.90 indicates mild to moderate peripheral arterial disease. Normal is 0.91 to 1.30.'),
    (v_topic_id, '126: An ABI > 1.30 is generally considered indicative of:', '["Excellent athletic conditioning", "Severe arterial occlusion", "Calcified, incompressible vessels (often seen in diabetes or renal failure)", "Venous insufficiency"]', 2, 'Arteries that are heavily calcified (Monckeberg medial sclerosis) cannot be compressed by the blood pressure cuff, yielding artificially high ABI readings.'),
    (v_topic_id, '127: During a vascular exam, the examiner performs the Allen test. This test assesses the patency of which structures?', '["The femoral and popliteal arteries", "The radial and ulnar arteries and the palmar arch", "The deep veins of the calf", "The carotid arteries"]', 1, 'The Allen test is performed before placing a radial arterial line to ensure collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery is patent.'),
    (v_topic_id, '128: When auscultating the abdomen of a patient with severe, poorly controlled hypertension, the examiner hears a bruit just above and lateral to the umbilicus. This suggests:', '["An abdominal aortic aneurysm", "Renal artery stenosis", "Mesenteric ischemia", "Cirrhosis"]', 1, 'A systolic-diastolic bruit in the epigastrium or upper quadrants is highly specific for renal artery stenosis, a cause of secondary hypertension.'),
    (v_topic_id, '129: The presence of visible, distended, engorged veins radiating from the umbilicus is known as:', '["Caput medusae", "Spider angiomata", "Palmar erythema", "Linea nigra"]', 0, 'Caput medusae is a sign of severe portal hypertension, caused by the recanalization of the umbilical vein shunting blood into the abdominal wall veins.'),
    (v_topic_id, '130: A male patient presents with breast enlargement. The examiner palpates a firm, rubbery, tender disk of tissue concentrically located beneath the areola. This finding is consistent with:', '["Pseudogynecomastia (adipose tissue)", "True gynecomastia (glandular tissue)", "Breast cancer", "Mastitis"]', 1, 'True gynecomastia is characterized by a palpable, firm disk of glandular tissue beneath the areola, whereas pseudogynecomastia is simply diffuse fat deposition without the firm disk.'),
    (v_topic_id, '131: When performing a digital rectal examination (DRE) on a male patient, a rock-hard, nodular, asymmetrical prostate gland is highly suspicious for:', '["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)", "Acute prostatitis", "Prostate cancer", "Normal aging"]', 2, 'A hard, irregular, and nodular prostate on DRE is the classic presentation of prostate cancer. BPH typically feels smooth, enlarged, and elastic.'),
    (v_topic_id, '132: Exquisite tenderness, swelling, and a "boggy" feel upon palpation of the prostate during a DRE is characteristic of:', '["Prostate cancer", "Acute bacterial prostatitis", "BPH", "Normal prostate"]', 1, 'An acutely infected prostate is swollen, exquisitely tender, and warm/boggy. Vigorous massage should be avoided to prevent bacteremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '133: The cremasteric reflex is elicited by lightly stroking the superior and medial part of the thigh. The normal response is elevation of the testis on the same side. Which spinal nerve roots are tested?', '["L1, L2", "L3, L4", "S1, S2", "S3, S4"]', 0, 'The cremasteric reflex tests the genitofemoral nerve, primarily derived from L1 and L2.'),
    (v_topic_id, '134: A male patient presents with a painless, cystic mass above the testis that transilluminates. Diagnosis?', '["Testicular tumor", "Varicocele", "Hydrocele", "Spermatocele"]', 3, 'A spermatocele is a benign, fluid-filled cyst located above and posterior to the testis that transilluminates. A hydrocele surrounds the entire testis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '135: A patient has a scrotal mass that feels like a "bag of worms" and increases in size with standing or Valsalva. It does not transilluminate. Diagnosis?', '["Hydrocele", "Varicocele", "Epididymitis", "Testicular torsion"]', 1, 'A varicocele is a dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus, most commonly on the left side, feeling classically like a "bag of worms."'),
    (v_topic_id, '136: During a pelvic examination, cervical motion tenderness (CMT), also known as "chandelier sign," is highly indicative of:', '["Endometriosis", "Ovarian cyst rupture", "Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)", "Uterine fibroids"]', 2, 'CMT occurs because moving the cervix stretches the inflamed adnexal structures, classically seen in PID.'),
    (v_topic_id, '137: The examiner palpates the adnexa and feels a firm, irregular, enlarged uterus. This is the classic physical exam finding for:', '["Endometrial cancer", "Uterine leiomyomas (fibroids)", "Ovarian cancer", "Cervical cancer"]', 1, 'Fibroids typically present as an enlarged, firm, irregularly contoured uterus on bimanual exam.'),
    (v_topic_id, '138: Upon speculum exam of the vagina, a bulging of the anterior vaginal wall is observed when the patient bears down. This is termed a:', '["Rectocele", "Enterocele", "Cystocele", "Uterine prolapse"]', 2, 'A cystocele is a prolapse of the bladder into the anterior vaginal wall.'),
    (v_topic_id, '139: In assessing the sensory system, testing temperature sensation utilizes the same spinal cord tract as testing:', '["Proprioception", "Vibration", "Pain (pinprick)", "Light touch"]', 2, 'Pain and temperature both travel in the lateral spinothalamic tract.'),
    (v_topic_id, '140: A patient has lost vibration and proprioception sensation in their lower extremities, but pain and temperature sensation are completely intact. Which spinal cord tract is damaged?', '["Lateral spinothalamic tract", "Anterior spinothalamic tract", "Corticospinal tract", "Dorsal columns (Posterior columns)"]', 3, 'The dorsal columns carry vibration, fine touch, and conscious proprioception. Selective damage to this tract (e.g., from B12 deficiency or neurosyphilis) impairs these specific modalities.'),
    (v_topic_id, '141: What is the defining feature of a lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion compared to an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion?', '["Spasticity", "Hyperreflexia", "Fasciculations and significant muscle atrophy", "Positive Babinski sign"]', 2, 'LMN lesions cause flaccid paralysis, absent/decreased reflexes, profound atrophy, and fasciculations. UMN lesions cause spasticity, hyperreflexia, and Babinski signs.'),
    (v_topic_id, '142: During the finger-to-nose test, the examiner asks the patient to touch their nose and then the examiner''s finger. The examiner moves their finger to different locations. What is the primary purpose of moving the target?', '["To test the visual fields simultaneously", "To test for intention tremor and dysmetria by requiring adjustments in trajectory", "To test muscle strength", "To test for resting tremor"]', 1, 'Moving the target forces the cerebellum to continuously calculate and adjust the trajectory, maximizing the detection of dysmetria (past-pointing) and intention tremor.'),
    (v_topic_id, '143: A patient exhibits nystagmus (involuntary eye movements). When the patient looks to the right, the fast phase of the nystagmus beats to the right. This is called:', '["Right-beating nystagmus", "Left-beating nystagmus", "Vertical nystagmus", "Pendular nystagmus"]', 0, 'Nystagmus is named for the direction of the fast (corrective) phase.'),
    (v_topic_id, '144: In evaluating a comatose patient, the examiner performs the oculocephalic reflex ("doll''s eyes" maneuver). What is the NORMAL response in a patient with an intact brainstem?', '["The eyes remain fixed mid-orbit as the head is turned", "The eyes move in the opposite direction of the head turn, maintaining forward gaze", "The eyes move in the same direction as the head turn", "The pupils dilate"]', 1, 'In an intact brainstem, the vestibular system compensates for head movement by moving the eyes in the opposite direction, as if the patient is staring at a fixed point (positive doll''s eyes).'),
    (v_topic_id, '145: A patient complains of vertigo. The examiner rapidly lays the patient backward with the head turned 45 degrees, and the head hangs off the edge of the bed. This is the:', '["Epley maneuver", "Romberg test", "Dix-Hallpike maneuver", "HINTS exam"]', 2, 'The Dix-Hallpike maneuver is used to diagnose Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV) by provoking the characteristic nystagmus.'),
    (v_topic_id, '146: What is the classic finding on the Dix-Hallpike maneuver in a patient with BPPV?', '["Immediate, continuous, down-beating nystagmus", "Delayed (latent), transient, fatiguable upbeat-torsional nystagmus", "Lack of any eye movement", "Pupillary constriction"]', 1, 'Peripheral vertigo (BPPV) classically features nystagmus with a latency of a few seconds, which adapts (fatigues) after 10-20 seconds and has a torsional component.'),
    (v_topic_id, '147: When testing the corneal reflex, touching the right cornea causes the right eye to blink, but the left eye does NOT blink. Assuming the cranial nerves were previously tested, where is the lesion?', '["Right CN V1", "Right CN VII", "Left CN VII", "Left CN V1"]', 2, 'The sensory input (Right V1) works because the right eye blinks. The motor output to the left eye (Left CN VII) is failing, so the left eye does not blink (consensual reflex absent).'),
    (v_topic_id, '148: A patient is unable to shrug their shoulders against resistance. This indicates a lesion of:', '["The accessory nerve (CN XI)", "The vagus nerve (CN X)", "The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)", "The facial nerve (CN VII)"]', 0, 'CN XI innervates the trapezius (shoulder shrug) and the sternocleidomastoid (head turning).'),
    (v_topic_id, '149: The examiner asks the patient to clench their jaw and palpates the masseter and temporalis muscles. This tests the motor function of which nerve?', '["Facial nerve (CN VII)", "Trigeminal nerve (CN V)", "Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"]', 1, 'The mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (V3) provides motor innervation to the muscles of mastication.'),
    (v_topic_id, '150: A patient has lost the sense of taste on the anterior two-thirds of their tongue. Which cranial nerve is affected?', '["Trigeminal (CN V)", "Facial (CN VII)", "Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)", "Vagus (CN X)"]', 1, 'The chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) supplies taste to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue. General sensation (touch/pain) to this area is supplied by CN V3.'),
    (v_topic_id, '151: Taste to the posterior one-third of the tongue is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (CN V)", "Facial (CN VII)", "Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)", "Hypoglossal (CN XII)"]', 2, 'CN IX supplies both taste and general sensation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue.'),
    (v_topic_id, '152: When examining the hands of a patient with osteoarthritis, the examiner notes bony nodules at the Distal Interphalangeal (DIP) joints. These are called:', '["Bouchard nodes", "Heberden nodes", "Rheumatoid nodules", "Tophi"]', 1, 'Heberden nodes are osteophytes at the DIP joints, classic for OA. Bouchard nodes are at the PIP joints.'),
    (v_topic_id, '153: A patient with rheumatoid arthritis often develops ulnar deviation of the fingers at which joints?', '["Distal Interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Proximal Interphalangeal (PIP) joints", "Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints", "Carpometacarpal (CMC) joints"]', 2, 'Ulnar deviation and subluxation at the MCP joints are classic late deformities in RA.'),
    (v_topic_id, '154: A "swan neck" deformity of the fingers consists of:', '["Hyperextension of the PIP joint and flexion of the DIP joint", "Flexion of the PIP joint and hyperextension of the DIP joint", "Fixed flexion of the MCP joint", "Ulnar deviation of all digits"]', 0, 'Swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension, DIP flexion) is a classic deformity seen in advanced rheumatoid arthritis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '155: A "boutonniere" deformity consists of:', '["Hyperextension of the PIP joint and flexion of the DIP joint", "Flexion of the PIP joint and hyperextension of the DIP joint", "Subluxation of the CMC joint", "Swelling of the MCP joints"]', 1, 'The boutonniere deformity is the opposite of the swan neck, involving PIP flexion and DIP hyperextension.'),
    (v_topic_id, '156: Palpation of the medial epicondyle of the humerus elicits severe pain. This is known as:', '["Tennis elbow (Lateral epicondylitis)", "Golfer''s elbow (Medial epicondylitis)", "Olecranon bursitis", "Cubital tunnel syndrome"]', 1, 'Medial epicondylitis ("golfer''s elbow") involves inflammation of the common flexor tendon origin.'),
    (v_topic_id, '157: A pediatric patient presents with a fever of 103F, a "strawberry tongue," cracked red lips, and bilateral conjunctival injection without exudate. This constellation is classic for:', '["Scarlet fever", "Kawasaki disease", "Measles", "Infectious mononucleosis"]', 1, 'These are classic criteria for Kawasaki disease, an acute vasculitis that requires prompt treatment to prevent coronary artery aneurysms.'),
    (v_topic_id, '158: Koplik spots (tiny white spots on the buccal mucosa) are a pathognomonic physical finding in the prodromal phase of:', '["Rubella", "Mumps", "Measles (Rubeola)", "Varicella"]', 2, 'Koplik spots are the highly specific enanthem of measles, typically appearing 1-2 days before the maculopapular rash.'),
    (v_topic_id, '159: During an infant''s examination, the examiner notes that the anterior fontanelle is deeply sunken. This is a sign of:', '["Increased intracranial pressure", "Meningitis", "Dehydration", "Craniosynostosis"]', 2, 'A sunken anterior fontanelle is a reliable sign of significant dehydration in an infant. A bulging fontanelle suggests increased ICP.'),
    (v_topic_id, '160: When does the anterior fontanelle normally close in a child?', '["By 2 months of age", "Between 9 and 18 months of age", "Between 2 and 3 years of age", "It never fully closes"]', 1, 'The posterior fontanelle closes around 2 months, but the larger anterior fontanelle remains open until 9-18 months to accommodate rapid brain growth.'),
    (v_topic_id, '161: A newborn has a swelling on the head that does NOT cross the cranial suture lines. This is a:', '["Caput succedaneum", "Cephalohematoma", "Subgaleal hemorrhage", "Encephalocele"]', 1, 'A cephalohematoma is a subperiosteal hemorrhage, meaning the blood is trapped under the periosteum of a single skull bone and therefore cannot cross the suture lines.'),
    (v_topic_id, '162: A newborn''s head swelling DOES cross the cranial suture lines. It is soft and boggy. This is a:', '["Caput succedaneum", "Cephalohematoma", "Epidural hematoma", "Skull fracture"]', 0, 'Caput succedaneum is edema of the scalp tissue above the periosteum, so it freely crosses suture lines and typically resolves in a few days.'),
    (v_topic_id, '163: To evaluate for strabismus (misalignment of the eyes) in a child, the examiner shines a light at the bridge of the nose and observes the reflection on the corneas. This is the:', '["Cover-uncover test", "Hirschberg test (Corneal light reflex)", "Red reflex test", "Visual acuity test"]', 1, 'The Hirschberg test checks for symmetric reflection of light on the corneas; asymmetric reflection indicates strabismus.'),
    (v_topic_id, '164: An absent red reflex (leukocoria or a white pupil) in an infant''s eye is an urgent finding that strongly necessitates evaluation for:', '["Glaucoma", "Retinoblastoma", "Conjunctivitis", "Astigmatism"]', 1, 'Leukocoria can indicate retinoblastoma (a malignant intraocular tumor) or congenital cataracts, both requiring immediate pediatric ophthalmology referral.'),
    (v_topic_id, '165: A child presents with an earache. The examiner pulls the pinna upward and backward to insert the otoscope. Why is this maneuver performed?', '["To test for pain indicating otitis externa", "To straighten the external auditory canal for better visualization", "To unblock a cerumen impaction", "To pop the tympanic membrane"]', 1, 'In adults and older children, the canal curves anteriorly and inferiorly. Pulling the pinna upward, backward, and slightly outward straightens it.'),
    (v_topic_id, '166: In children under 3 years old, how should the pinna be pulled to straighten the ear canal?', '["Upward and backward", "Downward and backward", "Straight outward", "Straight forward"]', 1, 'Because of the different angle of the infant canal, pulling downward and backward is required to straighten it.'),
    (v_topic_id, '167: During palpation of a lymph node, which characteristic is most indicative of an acute infectious process?', '["Hard and fixed", "Soft, tender, and mobile", "Rubbery, non-tender, and matted", "Pulsatile"]', 1, 'Infections typically cause reactive lymphadenitis, making nodes enlarged, soft (or firm), tender, and freely mobile.'),
    (v_topic_id, '168: A patient has "matted" lymph nodes (multiple nodes connected together that move as a unit). This is most commonly seen in:', '["Acute viral pharyngitis", "Tuberculosis or lymphoma", "Normal aging", "Lipomas"]', 1, 'Matted nodes occur when inflammation or malignancy breaks through the node capsule, causing them to adhere to one another. It is a concerning finding.'),
    (v_topic_id, '169: A patient presents with a rapid pulse. The examiner notes that the pulse amplitude decreases significantly during inspiration. What is this called, and what does it suggest?', '["Pulsus alternans; severe heart failure", "Pulsus paradoxus; cardiac tamponade or severe asthma", "Pulsus parvus et tardus; aortic stenosis", "Water-hammer pulse; aortic regurgitation"]', 1, 'Pulsus paradoxus is an abnormally large drop in systolic BP and pulse amplitude during inspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, '170: A pulse described as "parvus et tardus" (weak and delayed) is the classic physical exam finding of:', '["Aortic regurgitation", "Mitral stenosis", "Aortic stenosis", "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"]', 2, 'The fixed obstruction of severe aortic stenosis restricts stroke volume (parvus) and prolongs ejection time (tardus), blunting the carotid upstroke.'),
    (v_topic_id, '171: A patient has a bifid (double-peaked) pulse. This can be normal, but is classically accentuated in:', '["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)", "Mitral valve prolapse", "Aortic stenosis", "Atrial fibrillation"]', 0, 'HCM can cause a bisferiens (bifid) pulse due to initial rapid ejection followed by dynamic outflow tract obstruction and then continued ejection.'),
    (v_topic_id, '172: When testing a patient''s visual acuity with a Snellen chart, the patient reads the 20/40 line. What does this mean?', '["The patient sees at 40 feet what a normal eye sees at 20 feet", "The patient sees at 20 feet what a normal eye sees at 40 feet", "The patient has lost 20% of their vision", "The patient requires reading glasses of +4.0 strength"]', 1, 'The first number is the testing distance (20 feet). The second is the distance at which a person with normal vision could read that same line.'),
    (v_topic_id, '173: A 60-year-old patient complains of gradual, bilateral loss of central vision, but their peripheral vision is completely intact. Diagnosis?', '["Glaucoma", "Macular degeneration", "Cataracts", "Retinal detachment"]', 1, 'The macula is responsible for central, high-acuity vision. Macular degeneration destroys this central field while sparing the periphery.'),
    (v_topic_id, '174: A patient complains of gradual loss of peripheral vision, creating "tunnel vision." The optic disc shows increased cupping. Diagnosis?', '["Macular degeneration", "Open-angle glaucoma", "Cataracts", "Diabetic retinopathy"]', 1, 'Glaucoma damages the optic nerve (seen as cupping), beginning with the peripheral visual fields and working inward.'),
    (v_topic_id, '175: A patient describes a sudden, painless loss of vision in one eye, described as "a curtain coming down" over their vision. Diagnosis?', '["Acute angle-closure glaucoma", "Retinal detachment", "Optic neuritis", "Central retinal artery occlusion"]', 1, 'The sensation of a dark curtain falling over the visual field is the classic description of a retinal detachment.'),
    (v_topic_id, '176: Upon fundoscopy, the examiner sees "cotton-wool spots," flame hemorrhages, and hard exudates. These findings are hallmarks of:', '["Hypertensive or diabetic retinopathy", "Glaucoma", "Macular degeneration", "Retinal detachment"]', 0, 'These findings represent microvascular damage, ischemia, and lipid leakage, typical of uncontrolled diabetes or hypertension.'),
    (v_topic_id, '177: What is a pterygium?', '["A yellowish nodule on the bulbar conjunctiva that does not cross the limbus", "A fleshy, triangular growth of conjunctiva that crosses the limbus onto the cornea", "An infection of the meibomian gland", "A swelling of the lacrimal sac"]', 1, 'A pterygium is a benign growth of the conjunctiva onto the cornea, usually caused by chronic UV light and wind exposure.'),
    (v_topic_id, '178: A pinguecula differs from a pterygium because it:', '["Is always malignant", "Does not extend onto the cornea", "Causes severe pain", "Is filled with pus"]', 1, 'A pinguecula is a yellowish nodule on the conjunctiva that stops at the limbus and does not invade the cornea.'),
    (v_topic_id, '179: A painful, tender, red nodule at the margin of the eyelid is a:', '["Chalazion", "Hordeolum (Stye)", "Xanthelasma", "Blepharitis"]', 1, 'A hordeolum is an acute, painful staphylococcal infection of an eyelash follicle or meibomian gland. A chalazion is a painless, chronic granuloma of the meibomian gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, '180: A patient has yellowish, slightly raised plaques along the nasal portion of both eyelids. These are xanthelasmas and are associated with:', '["Hyperlipidemia", "Hypothyroidism", "Renal failure", "Liver cirrhosis"]', 0, 'Xanthelasmas are deposits of cholesterol and are often seen in patients with lipid disorders.'),
    (v_topic_id, '181: A white ring around the perimeter of the cornea in an 80-year-old patient is called:', '["Cataract", "Arcus senilis", "Corneal ulcer", "Pterygium"]', 1, 'Arcus senilis is a benign lipid deposit common in the elderly. If seen in a young person, it strongly suggests hyperlipidemia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '182: During a lung exam, the examiner places the ulnar edge of their hands on the patient''s back and asks the patient to say "99." The examiner is testing for:', '["Egophony", "Tactile fremitus", "Diaphragmatic excursion", "Percussion resonance"]', 1, 'Tactile fremitus evaluates the transmission of low-frequency vocal vibrations to the chest wall. It is increased with consolidation and decreased with effusion/pneumothorax.'),
    (v_topic_id, '183: A patient has chronic, severe asthma. On physical exam, the chest appears hyperinflated. What is the expected measurement for diaphragmatic excursion?', '["Increased (> 5 cm)", "Normal (3-5 cm)", "Decreased (< 3 cm)", "It cannot be measured in asthma"]', 2, 'Because the lungs are already hyperinflated and the diaphragm is flattened, the diaphragm cannot descend much further during inspiration, resulting in decreased excursion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '184: An elderly patient with osteoporosis has a prominent forward curvature of the thoracic spine. This is called:', '["Scoliosis", "Lordosis", "Kyphosis", "Gibbus deformity"]', 2, 'Kyphosis (often termed a "dowager''s hump" in severe osteoporosis) is an exaggerated convex curvature of the thoracic spine.'),
    (v_topic_id, '185: An exaggerated inward curvature of the lumbar spine, common in late pregnancy, is called:', '["Scoliosis", "Lordosis", "Kyphosis", "Spondylolisthesis"]', 1, 'Lordosis is the normal inward curve of the lumbar and cervical spine, which can become exaggerated in pregnancy or severe obesity.'),
    (v_topic_id, '186: To test for scoliosis, the most effective screening maneuver is the:', '["Straight leg raise", "Adam''s forward bend test", "Patrick (FABER) test", "Trendelenburg test"]', 1, 'The Adam''s forward bend test makes any rotational deformity (rib hump) prominent and easy to identify.'),
    (v_topic_id, '187: The examiner asks the patient to stand on one leg. When the patient stands on the right leg, the left pelvis drops downward. This is a positive:', '["Romberg test", "Trendelenburg sign", "Gower''s sign", "Thomas test"]', 1, 'A positive Trendelenburg sign indicates weakness of the hip abductors (gluteus medius/minimus) on the stance leg (the right leg in this case).'),
    (v_topic_id, '188: A 4-year-old boy with suspected Duchenne muscular dystrophy uses his hands to "walk up" his own legs to stand from a seated position. This is known as:', '["Trendelenburg sign", "Gower''s sign", "Babinski sign", "Ortolani sign"]', 1, 'Gower''s sign indicates severe proximal muscle weakness of the lower limbs and pelvis, classic for muscular dystrophy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '189: The FABER (Flexion, Abduction, External Rotation) test, also known as Patrick''s test, is primarily used to evaluate pathology in the:', '["Knee meniscus", "Sacroiliac (SI) joint or hip joint", "Lumbar spine", "Cervical spine"]', 1, 'Pain in the groin during FABER points to hip joint pathology, while pain in the posterior pelvis points to sacroiliac joint pathology.'),
    (v_topic_id, '190: To test for a hip flexion contracture, the examiner has the patient lie supine and fully flex one knee to the chest. If the opposite (extended) thigh lifts off the table, the test is positive. This is the:', '["Ober test", "Thomas test", "Lachman test", "Apley test"]', 1, 'The Thomas test assesses for shortening or contracture of the hip flexors (primarily the iliopsoas).'),
    (v_topic_id, '191: The examiner taps lightly over the median nerve at the palmar crease of the wrist. The patient reports a tingling, "pins and needles" sensation shooting into the thumb and index finger. This is a positive:', '["Phalen''s test", "Tinel''s sign", "Finkelstein''s test", "Allen test"]', 1, 'Tinel''s sign is elicited by tapping directly over the nerve to provoke symptoms of nerve compression.'),
    (v_topic_id, '192: A patient holds the backs of their hands together with wrists fully flexed for 60 seconds to reproduce the numbness of Carpal Tunnel Syndrome. This is:', '["Phalen''s test", "Tinel''s sign", "Finkelstein''s test", "Froment''s sign"]', 0, 'Phalen''s maneuver increases pressure within the carpal tunnel, compressing the median nerve and reproducing symptoms.'),
    (v_topic_id, '193: A patient is asked to grasp a piece of paper between their thumb and index finger. As the examiner pulls the paper away, the patient''s thumb IP joint flexes because the adductor pollicis is weak. This positive Froment''s sign indicates a lesion of the:', '["Median nerve", "Ulnar nerve", "Radial nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve"]', 1, 'The ulnar nerve innervates the adductor pollicis. To compensate for its weakness, the patient uses the flexor pollicis longus (median nerve) to hold the paper, flexing the thumb.'),
    (v_topic_id, '194: The sensory distribution of the median nerve in the hand involves:', '["The palmar surface of the thumb, index, middle, and radial half of the ring finger", "The palmar surface of the pinky and ulnar half of the ring finger", "The dorsal web space between the thumb and index finger", "The entire dorsal surface of the hand"]', 0, 'This is the classic median nerve distribution affected in Carpal Tunnel Syndrome.'),
    (v_topic_id, '195: Numbness over the dorsal web space between the thumb and index finger specifically isolates sensory loss of the:', '["Median nerve", "Ulnar nerve", "Radial nerve", "Axillary nerve"]', 2, 'The radial nerve provides sensation to the dorsal web space. Radial nerve palsy (e.g., "Saturday night palsy") also causes wrist drop.'),
    (v_topic_id, '196: When grading pulses, a normal, expected peripheral pulse is graded as:', '["1+", "2+", "3+", "4+"]', 1, 'On a standard 0 to 4 scale (or 0 to 3 scale in some texts like Bates), a 2+ pulse is considered normal and brisk. 1+ is diminished, 3+ is bounding.'),
    (v_topic_id, '197: A patient has a capillary refill time of 4 seconds in their toes. This indicates:', '["Normal perfusion", "Poor peripheral perfusion", "A deep vein thrombosis", "A bounding pulse"]', 1, 'Normal capillary refill time is less than 2 seconds. A prolonged time (> 2 seconds) suggests poor arterial perfusion, shock, or severe hypothermia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '198: During an abdominal exam, the examiner presses their fingers deeply into the abdomen and then quickly releases the pressure. The patient feels severe pain upon release. This is called:', '["Rebound tenderness", "Guarding", "Rigidity", "Referred pain"]', 0, 'Rebound tenderness (Blumberg''s sign) is a classic sign of peritonitis. The sudden movement of the inflamed peritoneum upon release causes the pain.'),
    (v_topic_id, '199: To test the olfactory nerve (CN I), what is the proper technique?', '["Use a strong, noxious stimulus like ammonia to ensure they can smell it", "Ask the patient if their vision is normal", "Ask the patient to identify a familiar, non-irritating scent like coffee or vanilla with their eyes closed", "Inspect the nasal mucosa for polyps"]', 2, 'Noxious stimuli like ammonia stimulate the trigeminal nerve (pain receptors), not the olfactory nerve. Familiar, mild scents should be used.'),
    (v_topic_id, '200: A patient reports a sudden "worst headache of my life" (thunderclap headache). On physical exam, they have a positive Brudzinski sign. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Migraine", "Tension headache", "Subarachnoid hemorrhage", "Giant cell arteritis"]', 2, 'A thunderclap headache accompanied by meningeal signs (Brudzinski/Kernig) is the classic presentation of a ruptured cerebral aneurysm causing a subarachnoid hemorrhage.');
